Non-Commutative Geometry of Intelligence

Operator Algebraic Foundations of Contextual Learning and Correlation

Ouadi Maakoul

---

Abstract

Classical inference rests on the commutative algebra of bounded measurable functions, where all observables are jointly measurable and the order of observation is irrelevant. Yet cognitive experiments, distributed multi‑agent systems, and quantum information theory exhibit contextuality: the statistical correlations among observations depend on the sequence in which they are collected. This dissertation develops a rigorous mathematical framework in which context‑dependent inference is modelled as a dynamical process on non‑commutative probability spaces. The fundamental object is a pair (\mathcal{A},\omega) consisting of a unital \mathrm{C}^*-algebra (or von Neumann algebra) of observables and a faithful state encoding prior beliefs.

We show that Bayesian belief updating corresponds to non‑commutative disintegrations, with exact recovery governed by the modular automorphism group of the prior state and by the Petz recovery map; approximate inversion is controlled by the Fawzi–Renner inequality. For multi‑agent systems, the set of achievable correlations forms a convex body whose extremal points bound the informational advantage of non‑commutative learners. We introduce the Maakoul constant \mathfrak{M} as an operational measure of this advantage. For bipartite matrix algebras equipped with real Bell functionals, \mathfrak{M} equals the real Grothendieck constant K_G (with known bounds 1.676<K_G<1.782). When communication is constrained to a graph, \mathfrak{M}(G) detects the presence of cycles and interpolates between classical and quantum correlations. Crucially, the constant is tied to the fixed‑point structure of the learning map, making it a property of the inference dynamics rather than of the correlation geometry alone.

On the geometry of learning, we prove that gradient flows on state manifolds are geodesically convex with respect to the Fisher–Rao metric, and that free independence provides a structural design principle that can prevent the exponential decay of gradient variances—a phenomenon known as barren plateaus—without requiring ad hoc circuit architectures. In the infinite‑dimensional (Type II and Type III) setting, the modular automorphism group of the state furnishes a natural representation of the agent’s internal time. When the learning channel is modularly compatible, this internal clock is preserved, and the modular and gradient flows combine into a unified complex‑time evolution that conserves the agent’s contextual baseline.

All results are established within the finite‑dimensional tensor‑product model of quantum correlations. The broader algebraic landscape, including the commuting‑operator model and the implications of the MIP* = RE theorem, is discussed openly. The framework does not assume quantum hardware; rather, non‑commutative probability serves as a mathematical tool for modelling classical but contextual phenomena, unifying and extending finite‑dimensional quantum cognition to the full power of operator algebras.

