Chapter 3: Non‑Commutative Bayesian Inference

3.1 Classical Bayesian Inversion

In classical probability, given a joint distribution of random variables X and Y, the conditional distribution p_{X|Y=y} satisfies the disintegration formula

\mathbb{P}(X\in A, Y\in B) = \int_B p_{X|Y=y}(A) \, d\mathbb{P}_Y(y).

Bayes’ theorem expresses the posterior as the normalised product of prior and likelihood. In the algebraic language, the conditional expectation \mathbb{E}[f(X)\mid Y] is the orthogonal projection in the L^2 space of the joint law, and it is these algebraic structures that admit a natural non‑commutative generalisation.

3.2 Non‑Commutative Disintegrations

Definition 3.2.1. A channel from a C*-algebra \mathcal{A} to \mathcal{B} is a completely positive unital (UCP) linear map \Phi:\mathcal{A}\to\mathcal{B}. Channels generalise Markov kernels.

Definition 3.2.2 (Disintegration). Let \mathcal{A} and \mathcal{B} be finite‑dimensional C*-algebras and \omega a state on \mathcal{A}\otimes \mathcal{B}. A disintegration of \omega with respect to \mathcal{A} is a UCP map \mathbb{E}_{\mathcal{B}|\mathcal{A}}:\mathcal{B}\to\mathcal{A} such that

\omega(a\otimes b) = \omega_{\mathcal{A}}\bigl(a\, \mathbb{E}_{\mathcal{B}|\mathcal{A}}(b)\bigr) \qquad \forall a\in\mathcal{A}, b\in\mathcal{B},

where \omega_{\mathcal{A}}(a) = \omega(a\otimes\mathbf{1}) is the marginal state on \mathcal{A}.

When a disintegration exists, the map \mathbb{E}_{\mathcal{B}|\mathcal{A}} plays exactly the role of a conditional expectation: it pushes observables of \mathcal{B} backward to \mathcal{A} while preserving the joint correlations.

3.3 Exact Bayesian Inversion: The Petz Map

The main tool for non‑commutative Bayesian inversion in finite dimensions is the Petz recovery map, constructed purely from the GNS data of the channel.

Definition 3.3.1 (Petz Map). Let \Phi:\mathcal{A}\to\mathcal{B} be a UCP map and \omega a faithful state on \mathcal{A}. The Petz recovery map \mathcal{P}_{\omega,\Phi}:\mathcal{B}\to\mathcal{A} is defined using the GNS representation (H_\omega,\pi_\omega,\xi_\omega) of \omega and the adjoint \Phi^*:H_\omega\to H_{\sigma} (where \sigma=\Phi(\omega)) by

\mathcal{P}_{\omega,\Phi}(b) = \pi_\omega^{-1}\!\bigl(J_\omega\, \Phi^*(\, \pi_\sigma(b) \,) J_\omega\bigr) \qquad (b\in\mathcal{B}).

Here J_\omega and J_\sigma are the modular conjugations.

The Petz map is always UCP and satisfies \mathcal{P}_{\omega,\Phi}(\sigma)=\omega. It provides a canonical candidate for a Bayesian inverse.

Theorem 3.3.2 (Petz Recovery Condition). Let \mathcal{A},\mathcal{B} be finite‑dimensional C*-algebras, \Phi:\mathcal{A}\to\mathcal{B} a UCP map, and \omega a faithful state on \mathcal{A}. Set \sigma=\Phi(\omega). The following are equivalent:

1. There exists a Bayesian inverse \Phi^\dagger:\mathcal{B}\to\mathcal{A} (UCP) with \Phi^\dagger(\sigma)=\omega;
2. D(\Phi(\rho)\|\Phi(\tau)) = D(\rho\|\tau) for all states \rho,\tau in the orbit of \omega;
3. The modular automorphism group \sigma_t^\omega preserves \ker\Phi, i.e., \sigma_t^\omega(\ker\Phi)\subseteq\ker\Phi for all t\in\mathbb{R};
4. The Petz map satisfies \Phi\circ\mathcal{P}_{\omega,\Phi} = \mathrm{id}_{\mathcal{B}} on the support of \sigma.

Proof. (1)\Rightarrow(2). The existence of \Phi^\dagger forces equality in the data processing inequality for relative entropy, which is equivalent to (2). (2)\Rightarrow(3). Equality in data processing for the whole orbit implies, by a theorem of Petz (1988), that there exists a conditional expectation \mathbb{E}:\mathcal{A}\to\mathcal{A}^\Phi (the multiplicative domain) preserving \omega and commuting with the modular flow; hence \sigma_t^\omega maps the kernel of \Phi to itself. (3)\Rightarrow(4). Invariance of the kernel yields a normal conditional expectation from \pi_\omega(\mathcal{A})'' onto \pi_\omega(\mathcal{A}^\Phi)''; restricting its modular conjugate to \mathcal{B} gives \mathcal{P}_{\omega,\Phi}, which satisfies (4). (4)\Rightarrow(1). The Petz map itself is a UCP inverse. \square

This theorem shows that the possibility of exact Bayesian inversion is entirely encoded in the modular invariance of the channel. The Petz map has recently been implemented experimentally on trapped‑ion and NMR quantum processors (2025), confirming its practical relevance for inference tasks.

3.4 Modular Compatibility and Persistence of Subjective Time

Definition 3.4.1 (Modular Compatibility). Let \Phi:\mathcal{A}\to\mathcal{B} be a channel and \omega a faithful state on \mathcal{A}. We say \Phi is modularly compatible with \omega if there exists a one‑parameter automorphism group \alpha_t:\mathcal{B}\to\mathcal{B} such that

\Phi\circ\sigma_t^\omega = \alpha_t\circ\Phi \qquad \forall t\in\mathbb{R}.

Modular compatibility ensures that the channel respects the internal time evolution defined by the state.

Theorem 3.4.2 (Persistence of Subjective Time). If \Phi is modularly compatible with \omega, then for the posterior state \sigma=\Phi(\omega) the Petz map intertwines the modular flows:

\sigma_t^\omega \circ \mathcal{P}_{\omega,\Phi} = \mathcal{P}_{\omega,\Phi} \circ \sigma_t^\sigma \qquad \forall t\in\mathbb{R}.

Consequently, the agent’s internal clock, encoded by the modular group of the prior, is mapped consistently to the modular group of the posterior: learning does not disrupt the subjective arrow of time.

3.5 Approximate Bayesian Inversion and the Fawzi–Renner Inequality

When exact inversion is impossible, one can still quantify the fidelity of recovery. The celebrated inequality of Fawzi and Renner (2015) shows that the optimal recovery error is controlled by the quantum conditional mutual information. We state a version adapted to our algebraic setting and provide a more detailed sketch of the proof, following later simplifications by Sutter, Tomamichel and Harrow (2015) and Berta, Lemm and Wilde (2015).

Theorem 3.5.1 (Fawzi–Renner for Channels). Let \Phi:\mathcal{A}\to\mathcal{B} be a UCP map between finite‑dimensional C*-algebras and \omega a faithful state on \mathcal{A}. Set \sigma=\Phi(\omega). Then there exists a recovery channel \Psi:\mathcal{B}\to\mathcal{A} such that

F(\omega,\Psi\circ\Phi(\omega)) \ge \exp\!\Bigl(-\frac12\, I(A:C|B)_\rho\Bigr),

where I(A:C|B)_\rho is the quantum conditional mutual information evaluated on a purification of the channel. In particular, the optimal recovery map can be taken as a convex combination of rotated Petz maps:

\Psi_t(b) = \mathcal{P}_{\omega,\Phi}\bigl(\Delta_\sigma^{it}\,b\,\Delta_\sigma^{-it}\bigr).

Proof sketch. For each t\in\mathbb{R}, the rotated Petz map \Psi_t satisfies

\Psi_t\circ\Phi(\omega) = \Delta_\sigma^{-it}\,\omega\,\Delta_\sigma^{it}.

By unitary invariance of the fidelity, F(\omega,\Psi_t\circ\Phi(\omega)) = 1 for every t. Now choose a probability measure \mu on \mathbb{R} with positive density (e.g., a Gaussian distribution) and define the averaged channel \bar\Psi = \int_{\mathbb{R}} \Psi_t\, d\mu(t). Using the joint concavity of the fidelity and the monotonicity of the logarithm, one obtains

-\log F(\omega,\bar\Psi\circ\Phi(\omega)) \le \int_{\mathbb{R}} -\log F(\omega,\Psi_t\circ\Phi(\omega)) \, d\mu(t) = 0,

which only gives the trivial bound. The actual proof replaces the fidelity with the sandwiched Rényi relative entropy and uses a delicate complex interpolation argument (Fawzi & Renner, 2015). A significantly simplified proof by Berta, Lemm and Wilde (2015) constructs the recovery map directly from the rotated Petz family using an operator‑convex combination and pinching maps, leading to the above bound. We refer the reader to Sutter et al. (2015) for the universal version and to the simplified proof in Berta et al. (2015) for the complete technical details. \square

The infinite‑dimensional generalisation of this inequality is subtle; extensions to separable von Neumann algebras with a faithful trace are possible, but the full Type III case remains an open problem.

3.6 Modular Flow as Subjective Time in Type III Algebras

Definition 3.6.1. Given a von Neumann algebra \mathcal{M} with a faithful normal state \omega, the modular automorphism group \sigma_t^\omega describes a one‑parameter evolution of observables. We interpret x(t) = \sigma_t^\omega(x) as the evolution of the observable x relative to the agent’s internal clock.

Theorem 3.6.2 (Contextual Drift). For two observables x,y\in\mathcal{M} with [x,y]\neq 0, the joint probability of sequential measurements depends on the order:

\mathbb{P}_\omega\bigl(x(t_1)=a, y(t_2)=b\bigr) \neq \mathbb{P}_\omega\bigl(y(t_2)=b, x(t_1)=a\bigr)

precisely when the modular operator \Delta_\omega does not commute with the spectral projections of x and y. The difference is given by the commutator of the time‑evolved projections.

3.7 Unified Inference Flow: Coupling Modular and Gradient Flows

We now combine the internal modular evolution with the objective‑driven gradient flow into a single complex‑time dynamical system.

Definition 3.7.1 (Unified Inference Flow). Let \rho_t be a time‑dependent state. Define the complex evolution parameter w = t + i\beta:

· the real part t corresponds to the modular flow (internal processing, contextual drift);
· the imaginary part \beta corresponds to the gradient flow (belief update towards lower learning functional).

The total flow is generated by the complex vector field \mathcal{V} = \mathcal{V}_{\sigma^\omega} - i\,\nabla_g\mathcal{L}, where \mathcal{V}_{\sigma^\omega} is the vector field generating the modular automorphism group and \nabla_g\mathcal{L} is the gradient of the learning objective with respect to the Fisher–Rao metric g.

Theorem 3.7.2 (Conservation of Context). A learning update is context‑preserving if and only if the Lie derivative of the learning functional along the modular vector field vanishes:

\mathcal{L}_{\mathcal{V}_{\sigma^\omega}} \mathcal{L} = 0.

In that case, the modular flow and the gradient flow commute, and the agent’s internal time is a symmetry of the learning objective. The unified flow preserves the contextual baseline.

