Chapter 4: Geometry of Multi‑Agent Correlations

4.1 Correlation Tensors and Agent Systems

We consider a system of n learning agents, each holding a local algebra of observables. The global correlations are encoded in a joint state on the tensor product of these local algebras. We restrict ourselves throughout to finite‑dimensional matrix algebras \mathcal{A}_i = M_{d_i}(\mathbb{C}) and to the tensor‑product model of quantum correlations (the set \mathcal{C}_{\text{qs}}). This is the standard model used in quantum information theory and is exactly the class of correlations that can be realised by finite‑dimensional quantum systems.

Definition 4.1.1 (Agent Inference System). An n-agent inference system consists of:

1. finite‑dimensional C*-algebras \mathcal{A}_i = M_{d_i}(\mathbb{C}) (i=1,\ldots,n);
2. a joint state \omega on the minimal tensor product \mathcal{A}_1\otimes_{\min}\cdots\otimes_{\min}\mathcal{A}_n;
3. for each agent i, a set of measurement observables \{A_i^{(k)}\}\subset\mathcal{A}_i (usually projection‑valued measures).

Definition 4.1.2 (Correlation Tensor). The correlation tensor associated with the measurements is

C_{i_1 i_2 \cdots i_n} = \omega\bigl(A_1^{(i_1)}\otimes A_2^{(i_2)}\otimes\cdots\otimes A_n^{(i_n)}\bigr).

4.2 Convex Geometry of Correlation Sets

For a given arrangement of local algebras and measurement settings, the collection of all possible correlation tensors forms a convex set. We distinguish three nested sets.

Definition 4.2.1 (Correlation Sets). Fix n and local dimensions d_i. Define:

· \mathcal{C}_{\text{class}}: the set of correlation tensors arising from states that are convex combinations of product states (classical correlations);
· \mathcal{C}_{\text{quant}}: the set of correlation tensors arising from an arbitrary state on the tensor product \bigotimes_i \mathcal{A}_i (finite‑dimensional quantum correlations, \mathcal{C}_{\text{qs}});
· \mathcal{C}_{\text{NS}}: the set of correlations satisfying the no‑signalling constraints.

Theorem 4.2.2. For n\ge 2 and all d_i\ge 2, these sets are strictly nested:

\mathcal{C}_{\text{class}} \;\subsetneq\; \mathcal{C}_{\text{quant}} \;\subsetneq\; \mathcal{C}_{\text{NS}}.

The gap between \mathcal{C}_{\text{class}} and \mathcal{C}_{\text{quant}} is precisely the domain where non‑commutative learning provides a genuine advantage.

4.3 The Maakoul Constant

We now introduce the central quantitative measure of contextual advantage for multi‑agent inference. The constant compares the optimal value of a Bell functional over quantum versus classical correlations. To obtain the correct connection with the Grothendieck inequality, we restrict to Bell functionals with real coefficients—a choice that mirrors the classical, real‑valued nature of probability and does not sacrifice generality for the bipartite case, as explained below.

Definition 4.3.1 (Maakoul Constant). For an n-agent system with local algebras \mathcal{A}_i = M_{d_i}(\mathbb{C}), let the real Bell functional \beta be a tensor with real entries \beta_{i_1\cdots i_n}\in\mathbb{R}. Define

B_{\text{class}}(\beta) = \sup_{C\in\mathcal{C}_{\text{class}}} |\beta(C)|,\qquad
B_{\text{quant}}(\beta) = \sup_{C\in\mathcal{C}_{\text{quant}}} |\beta(C)|.

The Maakoul constant of the system is

\mathfrak{M}(\mathcal{A}_1,\dots,\mathcal{A}_n) = \sup_{\beta\neq 0} \frac{B_{\text{quant}}(\beta)}{B_{\text{class}}(\beta)}.

Remark on the real coefficient restriction. In the bipartite case with projective measurements, the quantum expectation value \langle\psi| A_x\otimes B_y |\psi\rangle for dichotomic observables is always real. A Bell functional with real coefficients is therefore evaluated on real inner products \langle u_i,v_j\rangle, and the optimal quantum strategy can be chosen to lie in a real Euclidean subspace of the complex Hilbert space. Consequently, the real Grothendieck constant is the appropriate constant for the analysis of real Bell functionals, following the original approach of Tsirelson (1980) and the explicit treatment by Fishburn and Reeds (1994). The complex Grothendieck constant (with known upper bound K_G^{\mathbb{C}}\le 1.405) governs Bell functionals with complex coefficients and is a separate quantity; we restrict to the real case for clarity and leave complex functionals for future work.

Theorem 4.3.2 (Grothendieck Equality for Bipartite Systems). For \mathcal{A}_1 = M_d(\mathbb{C}) and \mathcal{A}_2 = M_d(\mathbb{C}) with d\ge 2,

\mathfrak{M} = K_G,

where K_G is the real Grothendieck constant, known to satisfy 1.676 < K_G < 1.782.

Proof. By Tsirelson’s theorem (Tsirelson, 1980), every bipartite quantum correlation in the finite‑dimensional tensor‑product model can be written as C_{ij} = \Re\langle u_i, v_j\rangle for unit vectors u_i,v_j in a Hilbert space (or, since the Bell functional is real, directly as the real part of the inner product, which can be absorbed into the vectors). Thus

B_{\text{quant}}(\beta) = \sup_{\|u_i\|,\|v_j\|\le 1} \Bigl|\sum_{i,j} \beta_{ij} \,\langle u_i,v_j\rangle\Bigr|.

The classical value is B_{\text{class}}(\beta) = \sup_{|a_i|,|b_j|\le 1} \bigl|\sum_{i,j} \beta_{ij} a_i b_j\bigr| with real scalars. Grothendieck’s inequality (real case) asserts the existence of a finite constant K_G such that for every real matrix \beta,

\sup_{\|u_i\|,\|v_j\|\le 1} \Bigl|\sum_{i,j} \beta_{ij} \langle u_i,v_j\rangle\Bigr| \le K_G \sup_{|a_i|,|b_j|\le 1} \Bigl|\sum_{i,j} \beta_{ij} a_i b_j\Bigr|.

Hence B_{\text{quant}}(\beta) \le K_G\, B_{\text{class}}(\beta) for all \beta, yielding \mathfrak{M} \le K_G.

For the lower bound, the definition of K_G as the optimal constant implies that for any \varepsilon>0 there exists a real matrix \beta achieving the ratio K_G-\varepsilon. By embedding the corresponding vectors into a sufficiently large finite dimension d, this ratio is realised by a quantum correlation tensor in \mathcal{C}_{\text{quant}}. Therefore \mathfrak{M} \ge K_G-\varepsilon for every \varepsilon, giving \mathfrak{M} \ge K_G. Thus \mathfrak{M} = K_G. The CHSH functional provides the explicit lower bound \sqrt{2}, confirming K_G\ge\sqrt{2}. \square

While the connection between Grothendieck’s inequality and quantum nonlocality has been known since Tsirelson’s work and later elaborated by Acín, Gisin, and others (Acín et al., 2006), the novelty of the Maakoul constant lies in its operational embedding into the dynamics of learning. We now introduce a refined version that depends on the fixed‑point structure of the learning map.

Definition 4.3.3 (Operational Maakoul Constant). Let \Phi be a learning channel (UCP map) on the global algebra, and let \text{Fix}(\Phi) denote the set of states invariant under \Phi. The operational Maakoul constant is

\mathfrak{M}_{\text{ops}}(\Phi) = \sup_{\substack{\beta \text{ real}\\ \rho\in\text{Fix}(\Phi)}} \frac{|\beta(C_{\text{quant}}(\rho))|}{B_{\text{class}}(\beta)}.

This quantity measures the maximal contextual advantage that can be sustained by a learner whose inference dynamics have stabilised. Unlike the static Maakoul constant, \mathfrak{M}_{\text{ops}}(\Phi) is a property of the learning dynamics rather than of the correlation set alone; it can serve as a resource quantifier for distributed inference protocols.

Lemma 4.3.4. If the fixed‑point set contains a state with non‑zero quantum discord, then \mathfrak{M}_{\text{ops}}(\Phi) > 1.

4.4 Graph‑Structured Inference Systems

When agents can only communicate according to a graph, the achievable correlations are constrained by the topology. Let G=(V,E) be an undirected graph whose vertices are the agents and whose edges represent allowed communication channels. The Maakoul constant of the graph is defined by maximising over all possible assignments of local algebras and joint states that are consistent with the communication constraints (local operations and shared randomness along edges).

Definition 4.4.1. \mathfrak{M}(G) is the supremum of \mathfrak{M}(\mathcal{A}_1,\dots,\mathcal{A}_n) over all finite‑dimensional matrix algebras \mathcal{A}_i and all correlation tensors achievable under the communication flow defined by G.

Proposition 4.4.2. Let G be a graph.

1. If G is a tree, then \mathfrak{M}(G)=1.
2. If G contains a cycle, then \mathfrak{M}(G) \ge \sqrt{2}, and \mathfrak{M}(G) can reach K_G when G is complete.

Proof. (1) For trees, it is known that all no‑signalling correlations can be simulated by a classical local hidden‑variable model (a result that generalises Fine’s theorem to networks; see Fritz, 2012 for the triangle‑free case and the broader literature on network Bell inequalities). Hence no Bell violation is possible, and \mathfrak{M}(G)=1.

(2) If G contains a cycle, we can select a simple cycle of length at least 3. By embedding the bipartite CHSH scenario onto two non‑adjacent vertices along this cycle (with other agents simply transmitting measurement outcomes), we obtain a quantum correlation that yields a ratio of at least \sqrt{2}. For the complete graph, any bipartite quantum correlation can be directly distributed, and by Theorem 4.3.2 the maximal ratio K_G is achieved. \square

4.5 Multi‑Agent Learning Protocols and Convergence

We now formalise the idea of multi‑round learning protocols and how the set of achievable correlations grows with communication rounds.

Definition 4.5.1 (Learning Protocol). A learning protocol for n agents consists of:

1. initial private states \rho_i on local algebras \mathcal{A}_i;
2. a sequence of communication channels \Phi^{(t)}_{ij}:\mathcal{A}_j\to\mathcal{A}_i at each round t=1,\ldots,T;
3. a joint state \omega_T on \mathcal{A}_1\otimes\cdots\otimes\mathcal{A}_n\otimes\mathcal{B} (where \mathcal{B} is a public algebra) that is consistent with the sequential application of the channels.

Definition 4.5.2. Let \mathcal{L}_n(T) be the set of correlation tensors that can be generated by some learning protocol after T rounds of bidirectional communication (over a complete graph).

Trivially, \mathcal{L}_n(0) = \mathcal{C}_{\text{class}} (no communication, all correlations are classical). For T\ge 1, the set strictly contains \mathcal{C}_{\text{class}}. The behaviour as T grows is a fundamental question. We state the expected limit as a formal conjecture, supported by the known connection between the NPA hierarchy and multipartite quantum correlations.

Conjecture 4.5.3 (Interpolation by Communication). For a complete communication graph,

\mathcal{L}_n(T) \;\longrightarrow\; \mathcal{C}_{\text{quant}} \quad \text{as}\quad T\to\infty.

Evidence and proof strategy. The NPA hierarchy (Navascués, Pironio & Acín, 2007) characterises \mathcal{C}_{\text{quant}} as the limit of a nested sequence of semidefinite relaxations \mathcal{C}_{\text{quant}}^{(k)}, where k is the length of the words in the moment matrix. In a communication protocol with T rounds, each round allows agents to exchange measurement outcomes, effectively increasing the length of operator sequences that can be correlated. One can map the rounds of communication to the levels of the NPA hierarchy, showing that \mathcal{L}_n(T) \subseteq \mathcal{C}_{\text{quant}}^{(T)} and that the inclusion becomes tight asymptotically. The detailed formalisation of this mapping, using the framework of quantum extensive-form games (Moreno et al., 2020), remains to be carried out; a full proof would establish the conjecture. \square

The operational Maakoul constant for such protocols inherits the interpolation behaviour: as the number of rounds increases, the contextual advantage available to stable learning equilibria approaches the full quantum limit \mathfrak{M}=K_G for the bipartite case.

Chapter 5: Entropy Inequalities and Learning Dynamics

5.1 Entropy Functionals

We work primarily in the finite‑dimensional setting \mathcal{A} = M_d(\mathbb{C}) equipped with the unique normalised trace \tau. For a density operator \rho \in S(\mathcal{A}) the von Neumann entropy is

S(\rho) = -\tau(\rho \log \rho),

and the Umegaki relative entropy of \rho with respect to a faithful state \sigma is

D(\rho \| \sigma) = \tau\!\bigl(\rho(\log \rho - \log \sigma)\bigr).

Both are non‑negative, convex, and monotone under completely positive trace‑preserving maps. These functionals provide the foundation for geometric studies of learning dynamics.

5.2 Free Probability and Gradient Persistence

The central claim of this section is that a structural property of the observable algebra—free independence—can act as a design principle that prevents the exponential decay of gradients in high‑dimensional non‑commutative learning systems. We first recall the necessary notions from free probability.

5.2.1 Free Fisher Information and Logarithmic Sobolev Inequality

Let (\mathcal{M},\tau) be a tracial non‑commutative probability space. For a self‑adjoint random variable X \in \mathcal{M}, the free difference quotient \partial_X is defined on polynomials by

\partial_X(P(X)) = \frac{P(X)-P(Y)}{X-Y}\Big|_{Y=X},

where the fraction is interpreted as a polynomial in X (Voiculescu, 1994). The free Fisher information is

\Phi^*(X) = \|\partial_X\|_2^2 = \tau\bigl((\partial_X)^* \partial_X\bigr).

A key property of free Fisher information is its additivity for freely independent variables:

\Phi^*(X_1, \dots, X_n) = \sum_{i=1}^n \Phi^*(X_i).

Voiculescu’s free entropy \chi(\rho) is the non‑commutative analogue of Shannon’s differential entropy. The free logarithmic Sobolev inequality relates these two quantities. For a semicircular variable S with variance \sigma^2, one has (Biane, 1997)

\chi(\rho) \le \frac{1}{2\sigma^2} \Phi^*(\rho) + \text{constant}.

5.2.2 Gradient Persistence Under Free Independence

We now formalise the gradient flow on a state manifold generated by a learning objective \mathcal{L}(\rho) = \tau(\rho V) - \beta^{-1} S(\rho), where the potential V is a fixed self‑adjoint observable. In the classical setting, the variance of the gradient of such an objective can decay exponentially with the number of parameters, a phenomenon known as the barren plateau problem in variational quantum circuits (McClean et al., 2018). The following theorem shows that free independence provides a structural antidote.

Theorem 5.2.3 (Gradient Persistence via Free Independence). Consider a learning system whose state \rho_t evolves according to the gradient flow

\frac{d\rho_t}{dt} = -\nabla_g \mathcal{L}(\rho_t),

where g is the Fisher–Rao metric on the state space of a finite‑dimensional \mathrm{C}^*-algebra. Assume that the observables whose parameters are being optimised decompose into a family \{X_1,\dots,X_n\} that are freely independent with respect to the reference state (e.g., the tracial state), and that the free Hessian of the potential V satisfies \nabla^2_{\text{free}} V \succeq \lambda I for some \lambda > 0 (strong convexity in the free sense). Then:

1. The spectral gap \gamma of the non‑commutative Laplacian associated with the free diffusion generated by the flow satisfies \gamma \ge \lambda > 0.
2. The variance of the gradient of \mathcal{L} with respect to the relevant parameters obeys
   \operatorname{Var}(\nabla\mathcal{L}) = \Omega(1),
   i.e., it does not vanish exponentially with the number of variables n.

Proof sketch. The additivity of free Fisher information ensures that the Bakry–Émery curvature condition \Gamma_2(f) \ge \lambda \Gamma(f) holds on the free state space with a constant \lambda that is independent of n. This yields a Poincaré inequality for the gradient, and the bound on the variance follows directly. Details are given in Appendix A.6. ∎

The theorem as stated relies on the assumption that the gradient flow preserves the free independence of the generators throughout the evolution. In a generic gradient descent this is not automatically true; however, a growing body of work in deep learning theory demonstrates that free probabilistic structures can remain remarkably stable under gradient dynamics. Chhaibi, Daouda and Semmad (2021) showed that the Jacobian of deep neural networks is governed by free multiplicative convolutions, and that this structure persists during training, not merely at initialisation. Similarly, the thesis of Daouda (2022) analyses the spectral dynamics of covariance matrices under gradient flow using free probability tools, providing strong evidence that the free independence hypothesis is a physically relevant approximation for sufficiently structured architectures.

We therefore propose a design principle: neural network layers can be parameterised so that their generators are freely independent at initialisation and their algebraic structure is approximately preserved over a characteristic timescale that scales favourably with the system size. The precise quantification of the degradation of free independence under arbitrary gradient flows remains an important open problem; we outline a possible approach in Section 7.3.

5.2.3 Free Diffusions and Positive Ricci Curvature

The Bakry–Émery theory for free diffusions provides a geometric characterisation of convergence rates. The non‑commutative Ricci curvature of the state manifold equipped with the free Fisher metric can be shown to satisfy a uniform lower bound, as stated in the next theorem.

Theorem 5.3.3 (Positive Non‑Commutative Ricci Curvature). For the state manifold of a free probability space generated by a semicircular family \{S_1,\dots,S_n\} with variance \sigma^2, the non‑commutative Ricci curvature of the free Ornstein–Uhlenbeck semigroup satisfies

\operatorname{Ric}_{\text{nc}} \ge \frac{1}{\sigma^2}.

For a \lambda‑strongly convex free potential V, the corresponding perturbed semigroup has \operatorname{Ric}_{\text{nc}} \ge \lambda, with \lambda independent of n.

Proof. The free Ornstein–Uhlenbeck semigroup satisfies the Bakry–Émery criterion \Gamma_2(f) \ge \sigma^2 \Gamma(f) (Biane, 1997). The perturbation by a strongly convex potential preserves this inequality with a modified curvature constant, using standard arguments from the \Gamma_2 calculus (Arnold & Carlen, 2001). ∎

This result is fundamental because it guarantees exponential convergence of the gradient flow to the Gibbs state, with a rate that does not deteriorate as the number of variables grows. This is the geometric counterpart of the absence of barren plateaus.

5.2.4 Design Principles for Free Architectures

The theoretical results above translate into concrete guidelines for building non‑commutative learning systems that avoid vanishing gradients.

1. Initialisation. The generating elements of each layer should be drawn from a semicircular distribution (or more generally, a freely infinitely divisible law) so that they are asymptotically freely independent in the large‑n limit.
2. Layer structure. Each transformation should be implemented as a non‑commutative polynomial in the generators of the previous layer, preserving the free product structure. Concretely, this can be achieved by alternating random unitary operations drawn from groups with semicircular spectral measures.
3. Monitoring. During training, the free Fisher information of the layer outputs should be tracked; its decay signals a loss of free independence and a potential barren plateau. Regularisation terms that penalise the violation of free independence (e.g. by enforcing the vanishing of free cumulants of order ≥ 3) may stabilise the flow.
4. Comparison with other methods. Unlike tensor network architectures (Barthel & Miao, 2022) or local‑cost‑function designs, which mitigate barren plateaus by restricting the expressivity of the circuit, the free independence approach is an algebraic constraint that keeps the full dimensionality of the state manifold while enforcing a specific correlation structure. Recent comprehensive surveys (Cunningham & Zhuang, 2025) confirm that no single method is universally superior; the free architecture adds a rigorous, dimension‑free guarantee to the toolkit.

5.3 Geometric Flows and Geodesic Convexity

The Fisher–Rao metric g^{\text{FR}} on the state space S(\mathcal{A}) is the unique monotone metric that reduces to the classical Fisher information in the commutative limit. It is defined by

g^{\text{FR}}_\rho(X,Y) = \frac12 \operatorname{Tr}\!\bigl(X \mathcal{L}_\rho(Y)\bigr), \qquad
\mathcal{L}_\rho(Y) = \int_0^\infty e^{-t\rho} Y e^{-t\rho} \, dt.

Theorem 5.3.2 (Geodesic Convexity of Relative Entropy). For any faithful state \sigma, the function \rho \mapsto D(\rho \| \sigma) is strictly geodesically convex on the Riemannian manifold (S(\mathcal{A}), g^{\text{FR}}).

Proof. The integral representation of the relative entropy (Petz, 2008) and Lieb’s concavity theorem imply that the Hessian of \rho \mapsto D(\rho \| \sigma) is positive definite in every direction tangent to a geodesic. ∎

This result guarantees that gradient flows driven by the free energy converge to the unique minimiser without spurious local minima.

5.4 Non‑Commutative Langevin Equation and Exponential Convergence

Stochastic effects are captured by the non‑commutative Langevin equation, a Lindblad master equation that incorporates both Hamiltonian and dissipative dynamics:

\frac{d\rho_t}{dt} = -i[H,\rho_t] + \sum_j \Bigl(L_j \rho_t L_j^* - \frac12 \{L_j^* L_j, \rho_t\}\Bigr),

where the Lindblad operators L_j are chosen so that detailed balance holds with respect to the target Gibbs state \rho_\infty \propto e^{-\beta V}. The following theorem synthesises the geometric and stochastic aspects.

Theorem 5.4.2 (Exponential Convergence of the Langevin Flow). The non‑commutative Langevin flow minimises the free energy \mathcal{L}(\rho) = \tau(\rho V) - \beta^{-1} S(\rho). Moreover, if the generator \mathcal{L} satisfies a Poincaré inequality with spectral gap \gamma > 0 (which is guaranteed by the positive Ricci curvature of Theorem 5.3.3), then

\mathcal{L}(\rho_t) - \mathcal{L}(\rho_\infty) \le e^{-\gamma t} \bigl(\mathcal{L}(\rho_0) - \mathcal{L}(\rho_\infty)\bigr).

Proof sketch. The free energy decreases monotonically along the flow by the Spohn inequality (Spohn, 1978). The spectral gap translates the entropy production into a differential inequality for the free energy excess, yielding exponential convergence. ∎

