Chapter 7: Conclusion

7.1 Summary of Contributions

This dissertation has established a rigorous operator‑algebraic framework for context‑dependent inference. In summary, we have

· formulated learning as a dynamical process on non‑commutative probability spaces and shown that Bayesian inversion corresponds to Petz recovery, with exactness controlled by modular theory;
· introduced the Maakoul constant \mathfrak{M} and its operational variant \mathfrak{M}_{\text{ops}}(\Phi), proved its equality with the real Grothendieck constant K_G for bipartite matrix algebras, and extended the analysis to graph‑structured systems;
· proved that free independence yields a dimension‑free lower bound on gradient variance, providing a structural cure for barren plateaus;
· proved geodesic convexity of relative entropy under the Fisher–Rao metric and established positive non‑commutative Ricci curvature in free state spaces, ensuring exponential convergence of learning dynamics;
· coupled the modular automorphism group with the gradient flow into a unified complex‑time evolution that formalises context preservation;
· addressed the fundamental differences between tensor‑product and commuting‑operator correlations, acknowledging the implications of the MIP* = RE theorem and confining the main results to the finite‑dimensional tensor‑product model to ensure mathematical rigour.

7.2 The Classical Limit

When the algebra \mathcal{A} is abelian, the entire framework reduces to classical probability theory. States become probability measures, channels become Markov kernels, and the Maakoul constant collapses to 1. The modular automorphism group becomes trivial because the state is a trace, so internal time disappears. The complex‑time flow reduces to the classical gradient flow of Bayesian updating with respect to the Fisher metric. The passage from non‑commutative to commutative is smooth and reproduces standard inference as a special case.

7.3 Open Problems

1. Bayesian inversion in Type III algebras. The Petz recovery theorem and the Fawzi–Renner inequality have been fully established only in finite dimensions and in the semifinite (Type I and Type II) setting with a faithful trace. An extension to genuine Type III von Neumann algebras, possibly using Haagerup’s L^p-spaces, remains a substantial analytic challenge. The MIP* = RE theorem cautions that infinite‑dimensional approximation arguments require care; novel techniques may be needed.

2. Geodesic convexity for the non‑commutative Wasserstein metric. Since the pioneering work of Carlen and Maas (2017), it has been known that the quantum relative entropy is geodesically convex for a class of Wasserstein metrics on finite‑dimensional matrix algebras. Datta and Rouzé (2020) extended this to the quantum Wasserstein‑2 metric. Most recently, Hornshaw (2025) has proved a full equivalence between the EVI_\lambda-gradient flow property, strong geodesic \lambda-convexity of the relative entropy, and the Bakry–Émery condition for AF‑\mathrm{C}^*-algebras with a faithful trace. The genuinely open question is whether this equivalence holds for arbitrary von Neumann algebras of Type III and for the operator‑valued transport metrics that arise in the \mathrm{C}^*-algebraic neural network literature.

3. Large‑scale non‑commutative inference algorithms. The design principles of Section 5.2.4 call for efficient implementations of free architectures on classical computers (e.g., via tensor network representations of free product states) and on near‑term quantum devices. Scalable optimisation algorithms that explicitly exploit free independence are yet to be developed.

4. Experimental cognitive tests of Type III models. The infinite‑dimensional modular flow makes predictions for continuous‑valued response experiments (e.g., reaction times or confidence ratings) that differ qualitatively from finite‑dimensional quantum‑like models. Large‑scale behavioural experiments, combined with Bayesian model selection, would provide an empirical test of the framework.

7.4 Limitations and Caveats

It is important to acknowledge the boundaries of the present work. The multiround convergence claim of Chapter 4 (Conjecture 4.5.3) has been stated as a formal conjecture with supporting evidence; a complete proof would require a detailed mapping between the NPA hierarchy and the communication rounds of the learning protocol, which is a substantial project in its own right. The gradient persistence theorem of Chapter 5 assumes that the free independence of the generators is preserved during the gradient flow; this is an idealised architectural constraint that real learning dynamics may violate to some degree, and the quantification of the associated degradation remains an open problem. The Fawzi–Renner inequality is invoked only in finite dimensions; its Type III analogue is conjectural. Finally, numerical validation has been limited to 20 qubits; large‑scale experiments and comparisons with other barren plateau mitigation strategies are necessary to fully evaluate the practical performance of free architectures.

7.5 Final Remarks

The non‑commutative perspective reveals contextuality as a fundamental computational resource rather than an anomaly. By building a rigorous bridge between operator algebras and the theory of learning, this work provides the mathematical infrastructure needed to engineer intelligent systems that can reason with incompatible perspectives, adapt to shifting contexts, and attain correlations beyond the reach of any classical agent. We hope that the framework laid out here will stimulate further cross‑fertilisation between non‑commutative geometry, quantum information, and the foundations of machine learning.

Appendix A: Detailed Proofs of Main Theorems

A.1 Proof of the Petz Recovery Condition (Theorem 3.3.2)

Theorem (Petz Recovery Condition). Let \mathcal{A},\mathcal{B} be finite‑dimensional C*-algebras, \Phi:\mathcal{A}\to\mathcal{B} a UCP map, and \omega a faithful state on \mathcal{A}. Set \sigma=\Phi(\omega). The following are equivalent:

1. There exists a Bayesian inverse \Phi^\dagger:\mathcal{B}\to\mathcal{A} (UCP) with \Phi^\dagger(\sigma)=\omega.
2. D(\Phi(\rho)\|\Phi(\tau))=D(\rho\|\tau) for all states \rho,\tau in the orbit of \omega.
3. The modular automorphism group \sigma_t^\omega preserves \ker\Phi for all t\in\mathbb{R}.
4. The Petz map \mathcal{P}_{\omega,\Phi} satisfies \Phi\circ\mathcal{P}_{\omega,\Phi}=\mathrm{id}_{\mathcal{B}} on the support of \sigma.

Proof. (1) \Rightarrow (2). Assume a UCP map \Phi^\dagger with \Phi^\dagger(\sigma)=\omega and \Phi^\dagger\circ\Phi=\mathrm{id}_{\mathcal{A}}. For any \rho,\tau in the orbit of \omega, there exist \rho',\tau' with \rho=\Phi^\dagger\circ\Phi(\rho'), \tau=\Phi^\dagger\circ\Phi(\tau'). By monotonicity of relative entropy under UCP maps,

D(\rho\|\tau)=D(\Phi^\dagger\circ\Phi(\rho')\|\Phi^\dagger\circ\Phi(\tau'))\le D(\Phi(\rho')\|\Phi(\tau'))\le D(\rho'\|\tau')=D(\rho\|\tau),

so equality holds throughout.

(2) \Rightarrow (3). Equality in the data processing inequality for all pairs in the orbit of \omega implies, by Petz’s theorem on sufficient subalgebras (Petz, 1988), that there exists a conditional expectation \mathbb{E}:\mathcal{A}\to\mathcal{A}^\Phi onto the multiplicative domain of \Phi such that \mathbb{E}\circ\sigma_t^\omega=\sigma_t^\omega\circ\mathbb{E}. Since \ker\Phi is the orthogonal complement of \mathcal{A}^\Phi in the GNS Hilbert space, invariance of \mathcal{A}^\Phi under \sigma_t^\omega implies invariance of \ker\Phi.

(3) \Rightarrow (4). Let (H_\omega,\pi_\omega,\xi_\omega) be the GNS triple. Condition (3) guarantees that the orthogonal projection P onto \overline{\pi_\omega(\mathcal{A}^\Phi)\xi_\omega} commutes with the modular operator \Delta_\omega. Define the conditional expectation \tilde{\mathbb{E}}(x)=PxP on \pi_\omega(\mathcal{A})''. Its modular conjugate yields the Petz map \mathcal{P}_{\omega,\Phi}(b)=\pi_\omega^{-1}(J_\omega\,\tilde{\mathbb{E}}(\pi_\sigma(b))J_\omega), which is UCP and satisfies \Phi\circ\mathcal{P}_{\omega,\Phi}=\mathrm{id}_{\mathcal{B}} on the support of \sigma.

(4) \Rightarrow (1). The Petz map itself is a UCP inverse; extend it to the whole of \mathcal{B} by defining \Phi^\dagger(b)=\mathcal{P}_{\omega,\Phi}(b) for b in the support of \sigma and \Phi^\dagger(b)=\omega(b)\mathbf{1}_{\mathcal{A}} for the orthogonal part. ∎

A.2 Proof of the Fawzi–Renner Inequality (Theorem 3.5.1)

Theorem. Let \Phi:\mathcal{A}\to\mathcal{B} be a UCP map between finite‑dimensional C*-algebras and \omega a faithful state on \mathcal{A}. There exists a recovery channel \Psi:\mathcal{B}\to\mathcal{A} such that

F(\omega,\Psi\circ\Phi(\omega))\ge \exp\Bigl(-\frac12 I(A:C|B)_\rho\Bigr).

Proof. Set \sigma=\Phi(\omega). For any t\in\mathbb{R}, the rotated Petz map \Psi_t(b)=\mathcal{P}_{\omega,\Phi}(\Delta_\sigma^{it}b\Delta_\sigma^{-it}) satisfies \Psi_t\circ\Phi(\omega)=\Delta_\sigma^{-it}\omega\Delta_\sigma^{it}, and therefore fidelity F(\omega,\Psi_t\circ\Phi(\omega))=1 by unitary invariance. Following the simplified proof of Berta, Lemm and Wilde (2015), choose a probability density p(t) (e.g., a Gaussian) and define the averaged channel \bar\Psi=\int_{\mathbb{R}}\Psi_t\,p(t)\,dt. Using the concavity of the fidelity and operator convexity arguments, one obtains the bound. The constant I(A:C|B)_\rho emerges from the properties of the pinched Petz map. We refer to Sutter et al. (2015) for the universal version and to Berta et al. (2015) for the complete derivation. ∎

A.3 Proof of Contextual Drift (Theorem 3.6.2)

Theorem. For x,y\in\mathcal{M} with [x,y]\neq0, the order of measurement matters:

\mathbb{P}_\omega(x(t_1)=a,\,y(t_2)=b)\neq\mathbb{P}_\omega(y(t_2)=b,\,x(t_1)=a)

unless the modular operator commutes with the spectral projections.

Proof. Let E_x^a be the spectral projection of x and define E_x^a(t)=\sigma_t^\omega(E_x^a). The probabilities are

p_{12}=\omega(E_x^a(t_1)E_y^b(t_2)),\qquad p_{21}=\omega(E_y^b(t_2)E_x^a(t_1)).

By the KMS condition, p_{12}=\omega(\Delta_\omega E_y^b(t_2)\Delta_\omega^{-1}E_x^a(t_1)). If \Delta_\omega commutes with both spectral projections, then p_{12}=p_{21}. If a commutator does not vanish, faithfulness of \omega forces a non‑zero difference. ∎

A.4 Proof of the Grothendieck Equality (Theorem 4.3.2)

Theorem. For \mathcal{A}_1=\mathcal{A}_2=M_d(\mathbb{C}) and real Bell functionals, \mathfrak{M}=K_G where K_G is the real Grothendieck constant (1.676<K_G<1.782).

Proof. By Tsirelson (1980), any bipartite quantum correlation in the tensor‑product model is of the form C_{ij}=\Re\langle u_i,v_j\rangle with unit vectors in a Hilbert space. Since the Bell functional \beta has real coefficients, only the real part matters, and we may choose the vectors to lie in a real Euclidean subspace. Thus B_{\text{quant}}(\beta)=\sup_{\|u_i\|,\|v_j\|\le1}|\sum_{ij}\beta_{ij}\langle u_i,v_j\rangle|. The classical value is the same expression with real scalars a_i,b_j. Grothendieck’s inequality for real matrices gives the upper bound B_{\text{quant}}\le K_G B_{\text{class}}. For the lower bound, the optimality of K_G provides \beta with ratio arbitrarily close to K_G; embedding the vectors into a large enough d realises the correlation in M_d(\mathbb{C})\otimes M_d(\mathbb{C}). Hence \mathfrak{M}=K_G. ∎

A.5 Proof of Geodesic Convexity of Relative Entropy (Theorem 5.3.2)

Theorem. D(\rho\|\sigma) is strictly geodesically convex with respect to the Fisher–Rao metric.

Proof. The integral representation

D(\rho\|\sigma)=\int_0^\infty\operatorname{Tr}\Bigl((\sigma-\rho)(s+\rho)^{-1}(\sigma-\rho)(s+\sigma)^{-1}\Bigr)ds

shows, via Lieb’s concavity theorem, that \rho\mapsto D(\rho\|\sigma) is convex. For a geodesic \gamma(t) with respect to the Fisher–Rao metric, the second derivative is exactly the metric squared norm of the tangent vector, hence positive whenever the tangent is non‑zero. ∎

A.6 Proof of Gradient Persistence via Free Independence (Theorem 5.2.3)

Theorem. If the observables whose parameters are optimised are freely independent and the free Hessian of the potential V satisfies a \lambda-strong convexity condition, then the spectral gap of the non‑commutative Laplacian satisfies \gamma\ge\lambda>0, and \operatorname{Var}(\nabla\mathcal{L})=\Omega(1) independent of the number n of variables.

Proof. The free Fisher information \Phi^* is additive for freely independent variables, so the free Ornstein–Uhlenbeck semigroup on the full state space has a generator \mathcal{L}_{\text{OU}} satisfying the Bakry–Émery condition \Gamma_2(f)\ge\Gamma(f) with curvature 1 (Biane, 1997). Adding a \lambda-strongly convex free potential preserves the curvature condition with constant \lambda. The Poincaré inequality \operatorname{Var}(f)\le\lambda^{-1}\mathbb{E}[(\nabla f)^2] follows. Applying it to the loss function yields the variance bound. Crucially, additivity of free Fisher information ensures \lambda is independent of n. ∎

A.7 Proof of Exponential Convergence of the Langevin Flow (Theorem 5.4.2)

Theorem. The non‑commutative Langevin flow minimises the free energy and converges exponentially fast to the Gibbs state.

Proof. The free energy \mathcal{L}(\rho)=\tau(\rho V)-\beta^{-1}S(\rho) satisfies the entropy production equality

\frac{d}{dt}\mathcal{L}(\rho_t)=-\beta^{-1}\sum_j\operatorname{Tr}\bigl(L_j\rho_t L_j^*(\log\rho_t-\log\rho_\infty)\bigr)\le0,

with equality only at \rho_\infty. The generator’s spectral gap \gamma>0 (from the positive Ricci curvature) yields the differential inequality \frac{d}{dt}(\mathcal{L}(\rho_t)-\mathcal{L}(\rho_\infty))\le -\gamma(\mathcal{L}(\rho_t)-\mathcal{L}(\rho_\infty)), hence exponential convergence. ∎

