Appendix B: Background on von Neumann Algebras and Modular Theory

B.1 GNS Construction (Detailed)

Let \omega be a state on a C*-algebra \mathcal{A}. The form \langle a,b\rangle_\omega = \omega(b^*a) is positive semi‑definite. Set N_\omega=\{a\in\mathcal{A}:\omega(a^*a)=0\}; the quotient \mathcal{A}/N_\omega is a pre‑Hilbert space. Its completion is the GNS Hilbert space H_\omega. The representation \pi_\omega(a)(b+N_\omega)=ab+N_\omega is a *-homomorphism, and the cyclic vector is \xi_\omega=\mathbf{1}+N_\omega. Faithfulness of \omega implies N_\omega=\{0\} and injectivity of \pi_\omega.

B.2 Tomita–Takesaki Theory

Given a von Neumann algebra \mathcal{M} with a cyclic and separating vector \Omega, the operator S:a\Omega\mapsto a^*\Omega is closable. Its polar decomposition S=J\Delta^{1/2} yields the modular conjugation J (antilinear, J^2=I) and the modular operator \Delta=S^*S. The modular automorphism group is \sigma_t^\omega(x)=\Delta^{it}x\Delta^{-it}. Key properties:

· \sigma_t^\omega is a one‑parameter automorphism group of \mathcal{M}.
· J\mathcal{M}J=\mathcal{M}' (the commutant).
· The KMS condition: for any x,y\in\mathcal{M} there exists a bounded continuous function F_{x,y}(t) on 0\le \Im t\le 1, analytic in the interior, such that F_{x,y}(t)=\omega(x\sigma_t^\omega(y)) and F_{x,y}(t+i)=\omega(\sigma_t^\omega(y)x).

B.3 Conditional Expectations and Takesaki’s Theorem

Theorem (Takesaki, 1972). Let \mathcal{N}\subseteq\mathcal{M} be von Neumann algebras and \omega a faithful normal state on \mathcal{M}. A normal faithful conditional expectation from \mathcal{M} onto \mathcal{N} preserving \omega exists if and only if \mathcal{N} is invariant under \sigma_t^\omega.

