Appendix C: Essentials of Free Probability and Positive Ricci Curvature

C.1 Free Cumulants and the R‑Transform

In a tracial non‑commutative probability space (\mathcal{M},\tau), the free cumulants \kappa_n of a random variable X are defined implicitly by

\tau(X^n)=\sum_{\pi\in NC(n)}\prod_{V\in\pi}\kappa_{|V|}(X),

where NC(n) is the set of non‑crossing partitions. The R‑transform R_X(z)=\sum_{n\ge0}\kappa_{n+1}(X)z^n linearises free convolution: for freely independent X,Y, R_{X+Y}=R_X+R_Y. The semicircular distribution has R_S(z)=z.

C.2 Free Fisher Information

For a self‑adjoint X, the free difference quotient \partial_X acts on polynomials by

\partial_X(P(X))=\frac{P(X)-P(Y)}{X-Y}\Big|_{Y=X},

interpreted algebraically. The free Fisher information is \Phi^*(X)=\|\partial_X\|_2^2. Additivity: for freely independent X_1,\dots,X_n, \Phi^*(X_1,\dots,X_n)=\sum_i\Phi^*(X_i).

C.3 Free Entropy

Voiculescu’s microstates free entropy is

\chi(X)=\iint_{\mathbb{R}^2}\log|s-t|\,d\mu_X(s)\,d\mu_X(t)+\frac34,

with \mu_X the spectral distribution. The free log‑Sobolev inequality states \chi(\rho)\le\frac12\Phi^*(\rho)+\text{const}.

C.4 Positive Ricci Curvature for Free Diffusions (Theorem 5.3.3)

The free Ornstein–Uhlenbeck semigroup satisfies the Bakry–Émery condition \Gamma_2(f)\ge\Gamma(f) with curvature 1 (Biane, 1997). For a \lambda-strongly convex potential, perturbation yields \Gamma_2(f)\ge\lambda\Gamma(f), hence \operatorname{Ric}_{\text{nc}}\ge\lambda, independent of the number of generators due to additivity of free Fisher information.

