Appendix D: Tensor Network Representations for Non‑Commutative Inference

D.1 Matrix Product States

For a chain of N sites with local dimension d, an MPS is

|\psi\rangle=\sum_{s_1,\dots,s_N}\operatorname{Tr}(A_1^{s_1}\cdots A_N^{s_N})|s_1\cdots s_N\rangle,

where each A_i^{s} is a D\times D matrix. Mixed states are represented as Matrix Product Density Operators or via purifications.

D.2 Efficient Computation of the Petz Map

Given a prior state as an MPS and a local channel \Phi, the Petz map can be approximated variationally by minimising a fidelity loss over the space of MPS using DMRG or TEBD algorithms. The cost scales as O(ND^3) per update.

D.3 Scalability

For systems with low entanglement (bounded bond dimension D), the representation remains efficient. The free architecture design principle of Chapter 5 aims at keeping the bond dimension bounded by ensuring freely independent generators and avoiding long‑range correlations.

