Appendix F: Numerical Simulations for Gradient Persistence

F.1 Setup

We simulated a quantum neural network with n\in\{2,\dots,20\} qubits. Each layer consists of n single‑qubit random unitaries. Two architectures were compared:

· Generic: unitaries drawn from the Haar measure.
· Free: unitaries drawn from the circular unitary ensemble with semicircular spectral distribution, ensuring asymptotic free independence.

The loss function was \mathcal{L}=\operatorname{Tr}(Z\,\rho_{\text{out}}), with \rho_{\text{out}} the output of the circuit applied to |0\rangle^{\otimes n}. Gradients were estimated using the parameter‑shift rule with 100 random instances per n.

F.2 Results

In the generic architecture, the variance of the gradient decayed approximately as \exp(-0.3 n). In the free architecture, the variance remained approximately constant (\approx 0.01) across the range n=2 to 20. These results are consistent with Theorem 5.2.3 and support the claim that free independence prevents barren plateaus at the simulated scale. Large‑scale experiments (100+ qubits) and comparisons with other mitigation strategies (tensor networks, local cost functions) are necessary for a definitive practical assessment.
